+# Use signed cookies for sessions
+
+For whoever changes login, deploys or the database. Sessions use signed cookies because an accumulation of expired
+database sessions filled the disk and stopped Saturday pickup.
+
+## Context
+
+In 2023 larder moved from one server to two behind a load balancer, and sessions stayed in each server's memory.
+Members were logged out whenever a request reached the other server, and every deploy logged out everyone; the pantry
+volunteers stopped deploying during opening hours. Sessions moved to Postgres so all servers could read them and
+deploys would preserve them without adding a service.
+
+On August 3, the database disk filled up and larder was down from 09:40 to 12:50 during Saturday pickup. The
+sessions table held 2.1 million rows: expired sessions had never been deleted. Volunteers used a paper list, and
+eleven members left without their pantry orders because volunteers could not find them. Deleting expired rows
+restored service, but did not prevent a recurrence. [Issue 63](../../notes/issue-63.md) records the incident and
+the replacement shipped on August 9.
+
+Deploys must still preserve sessions, and the volunteers want to keep services and maintenance jobs few. Avoiding
+another session-table outage now also matters; losing individual server-side session revocation is acceptable for
+the pantry's [session lifetime](../features/sessions.md#lifetime).
+
+## Options
+
+- **Sticky sessions at the load balancer**: no code change; lost because a deploy still logs out everyone on the
+  restarted server.
+- **Redis as a session store**: fast and common; rejected in 2023 and again here because it adds a service the
+  volunteers would run only for sessions.
+- **Postgres with a nightly expiry cleanup job**: the previous store could be kept, but cleanup adds another job to
+  watch and leaves a database read on every request.
+- **Signed cookies**: chosen.
+
+## Decision
+
+Keep the member and expiry in a signed cookie that each web server checks with the shared key, implemented in
+[`larder/sessions.py`](../../larder/sessions.py). This removes session storage, its cleanup job and the per-request
+database query while preserving sessions across deploys. The sessions table has been dropped.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Expired sessions no longer accumulate in the database, and sessions need no additional service or cleanup job.
+Deploys preserve sessions when servers retain the shared key. This trades away
+[individual server-side revocation](../features/sessions.md#known-limits); changing the key has a
+[global logout impact](../features/sessions.md#signing-key).
